=== SIGNATURE ===
z_num(x)

=== COMMENT ===
Helper for required numeric arguments (counts, steps, row/column numbers, seeds): collapses a 1x1 array to a scalar (z_scalar) and converts a number stored as text ("5") to the number. Anything else - real numbers, booleans, non-numeric text, blanks, errors, larger arrays - passes through unchanged.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(x,
  LET(v, z_scalar(x), IF(ISTEXT(v), IFERROR(VALUE(v), v), v))
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Private helper: the numeric twin of z_scalar, for REQUIRED arguments that are numbers by nature (a count, a step, a distance, a row or column number, a seed). Optional arguments use z_opt instead, which also handles omitted / blank.

Why: case data and intermediate results often carry numbers as text - a TEXTSPLIT of "7000005;8000004", a cell formatted as text, a value typed with a leading apostrophe. Arithmetic coerces such text silently, but comparisons (n < 1), SEQUENCE / MAKEARRAY sizes and SWITCH / XMATCH on numeric codes do not, so a lambda can take the wrong branch or return one element without any error.

Behaviour:
  5, TOCOL(5), {5}   -> 5
  "5", TOCOL("5")    -> 5
  "abc", TRUE, blank, an error, a 2x3 array -> unchanged (an array of text numbers is converted element-wise)

It never changes the meaning of a value that is looked up in data; use it only where the argument is a number by definition.

Arguments:
  x - the argument to normalise.

Returns:
  A true numeric scalar when x is (or holds) a number; otherwise x unchanged.

Example:
  z_num("3")        ->  3
  z_num(TOCOL(3))   ->  3
  z_num("UL")       ->  "UL"
